{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 confines its distinguishing evidence to OOD/adversarial cases, but the problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation. OOD cases by definition fall outside that evaluation's distribution, so SRC_001 does not actually identify an evidence signal available in the single evaluation itself; it defers the discrimination to a shifted distribution the evaluation does not contain. A genuine single-evaluation signal would have to be internal to the original task set — e.g., item-level panel–expert disagreement patterns, correlation of checklist-step adherence with correctness, or calibration checks — rather than OOD probes that the single evaluation excludes.","counterexample":["Within one evaluation, compare per-item correctness conditional on full checklist adherence versus partial adherence: if high correctness occurs only when the checklist happens to match the answer regardless of adherence pattern, that indicates compliance; if correctness tracks step-level fidelity, that indicates genuine reliability gain — all without leaving the evaluation distribution."],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues for the panel's superiority by claiming the checklist externalizes the cognitive operations that constitute expert competence. But that is a non sequitur for the stated comparison: the question concerns trained non-experts, and a checklist that merely externalizes expert operations does not show that non-experts executing those operations match expert competence. If the operations encode tacit perceptual or inferential expertise that non-experts lack, the checklist externalizes the form of the steps without the underlying competence, so the distributed-averaging argument cannot establish superior reliability over the expert.","counterexample":["Consider dermatologic diagnosis of melanoma: the ABCDE checklist externalizes expert perceptual steps, yet trained non-experts applying it still underperform dermatologists on ambiguous lesions because boundary cases require pattern-recognition expertise the checklist cannot transmit."]}]}